  "worked_solution": "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\u20612x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
